1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,320
Hello.

2
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:06,260
Thank you guys for having me today.

3
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:09,100
I'll be filling in for Caroline today, obviously.

4
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:13,280
So I'll have a brief remarks here and then we'll get to your questions.

5
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:19,680
As you know, over the weekend the President announced this project, Freedom, and the goal of it

6
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:23,880
is to frankly, if you want to talk about it, it's to rescue like almost 23,000 civilians

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:29,680
from 87 different countries that are trapped inside of the Gulf and left for dead.

8
00:00:30,260 --> 00:00:33,440
in the Persian Gulf by this Iranian regime.

9
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:34,820
You know, for more than two months now,

10
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:37,140
these innocent sailors and commercial crew members

11
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:38,660
have been stranded out at sea

12
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:41,440
because Iran is conducting something that's not just criminal.

13
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:43,960
It's criminal for sure, but it's desperate and destructive,

14
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:45,860
this blockade of the Straits of Hormuz.

15
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:47,520
Nations from around the world,

16
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:49,720
the overwhelming majority of whom are not even engaged

17
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:51,780
in any military hostilities,

18
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:54,660
are now at risk not just of losing their cargo,

19
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:56,860
but the lives of their own citizens

20
00:00:57,400 --> 00:00:58,540
because of this blockade.

21
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:01,700
These ships, you know, you don't leave a ship out there for this long.

22
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:05,980
You start running out of food, you start running out of potable water, essential supplies,

23
00:01:06,020 --> 00:01:07,880
and they're at the mercy of this piracy.

24
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:09,940
What it is, it's piracy.

25
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:14,360
And not only that, but some of them have seen, you know, been open fire on

26
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:18,140
and rained down senseless attacks on several civilian ships already.

27
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:21,640
So, frankly, the way to put it is these are innocent bystanders.

28
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:25,920
These are countries that and ships and so forth that have nothing to do with any of this

29
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,440
and nonetheless are being caught in the middle of it and being held hostage merely because

30
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:30,820
Iran could do that.

31
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:35,900
Just because of just as the regime brutally slaughtered tens of thousands of their own citizens

32
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:41,220
for the crime of peaceful protest because they're unhappy with the quality of life or the lack

33
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:42,740
of quality of life in Iran today.

34
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:44,320
And so they're sitting ducks.

35
00:01:44,460 --> 00:01:44,800
They're isolated.

36
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:45,800
They're starving.

37
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:46,680
They're vulnerable.

38
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:51,800
And at least 10 sailors have already died as a result of the civilian sailors.

39
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:56,680
So already many nations privately and some publicly have asked the United States to help free their ships

40
00:01:56,940 --> 00:02:01,460
and to restore freedom of navigation in the Straits of Hormuz and this critical artery of global trade.

41
00:02:01,860 --> 00:02:04,400
And so President Trump, as he always does, stepped up.

42
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:06,680
and answer the calls for their help.

43
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:10,620
And he's directed the United States military to guide these stranded ships to safety,

44
00:02:10,620 --> 00:02:14,480
to provide a protective bubble under which they can operate and move product

45
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:17,120
and get themselves out of there and out of the harm's way.

46
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:19,600
And this is the first step towards reopening the strait

47
00:02:19,620 --> 00:02:23,880
and bringing this regime's last-ditch act of economic arson,

48
00:02:24,220 --> 00:02:25,480
bringing that to a close.

49
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:30,300
Only we're doing it, not only because we were asked, but because we're the only ones who can.

50
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:33,640
Only we have the power to sort of take the steps that we've taken now.

51
00:02:34,040 --> 00:02:38,060
Under this president, under President Trump, the United States will help our friends.

52
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:41,520
We're going to stand up to rogue regimes like the one in Tehran.

53
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:47,200
And we're going to be unashamed to use our power and our abilities to project military power

54
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:49,860
in the service of our national interests, above all else.

55
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:55,380
Now, what's really important for you to report and for everyone to understand is this is not an offensive operation.

56
00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:57,020
This is a defensive operation.

57
00:02:57,020 --> 00:02:58,340
And what that means is very simple.

58
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:00,920
There's no shooting unless we're shot at first.

59
00:03:01,580 --> 00:03:02,820
Okay, we're not attacking them.

60
00:03:02,820 --> 00:03:03,120
We're not.

61
00:03:03,420 --> 00:03:06,920
But if they are attacking us or they're attacking a ship, you need to respond to that.

62
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:10,420
You're not going to let some fastboat come up on a ship and shoot it up.

63
00:03:10,820 --> 00:03:13,060
We're going to respond to it, and we've been successful at it.

64
00:03:13,060 --> 00:03:16,640
I don't know what the exact number is, but I know a number of those fast boats have now been targeted.

65
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:19,240
And we'll continue to be if they pose a threat to our forces.

66
00:03:19,320 --> 00:03:20,220
We'll shoot down drones.

67
00:03:20,220 --> 00:03:21,200
We'll shoot down missiles.

68
00:03:21,380 --> 00:03:22,780
But it's defensive in nature.

69
00:03:22,980 --> 00:03:23,980
This is defensive.

70
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:28,860
So if you hear stories about attacks and launching of firing back and forth, it's not back and forth.

71
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:31,140
We are only responding if attacked first.

72
00:03:31,140 --> 00:03:34,340
This is a defensive operation, and that's what's occurring here.

73
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:37,020
just about the importance of the Straits for a moment.

74
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:39,320
This is approximately a quarter of the world's oil trade,

75
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:42,200
along with significant volumes of fuel and fertilizer

76
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:45,020
that operate through the Straits of Hormuz.

77
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:47,620
The Iranian regime cannot be allowed to dictate

78
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:49,420
who uses this vital waterway.

79
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:51,960
I don't think this is also being reported enough.

80
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:52,780
Maybe you are reporting.

81
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:53,860
I don't read everyone.

82
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:54,900
I don't have many damn outlets here.

83
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:55,820
I don't know who you all are.

84
00:03:56,100 --> 00:03:57,620
But I mean, I know who some of you are,

85
00:03:57,620 --> 00:03:58,520
but I don't know who all of you are.

86
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:00,880
But I will say this about it.

87
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:03,480
This is an international waterway.

88
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:14,440
Okay, this is an international waterway and international law is very clear and I love it because everybody always talks about international law and this international law on this is very clear international waterways, no country can control them.

89
00:04:14,820 --> 00:04:22,500
There is no international law that allows you to say I'm going to put mines in an international body of water and I'm going to blow up ships that don't listen to us and try to go through.

90
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:24,000
That's what Iran is doing.

91
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:27,740
This is a criminal act and someone needs to do something about it.

92
00:04:27,820 --> 00:04:28,660
Something needs to be done.

93
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:32,520
It's completely illegal, completely illegitimate and completely unacceptable.

94
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:37,140
And that's why the United States military is guiding stranded commercial ships safely through the strait

95
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:43,120
and is working to restore freedom of navigation and putting an end to these efforts to hold the global economy hostage.

96
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:55,140
So far, as a proof of concept and as a proof of function, two U.S. flag merchant ships have successfully transferred the Strait of Hormuz in the first stages of this project, and they're now safely on their way.

97
00:04:55,460 --> 00:05:00,940
The U.S. military is deploying the necessary assets to extend this defensive umbrella over commercial shipping.

98
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:03,000
But there should be no mistake.

99
00:05:03,260 --> 00:05:05,880
And as I said this already, this is a defensive operation.

100
00:05:05,880 --> 00:05:07,100
I want to reiterate that point.

101
00:05:07,380 --> 00:05:08,840
This is important to understand.

102
00:05:08,900 --> 00:05:13,500
If no shots are fired at these ships and no shots are fired at us, we're not firing shots.

103
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:17,360
But if we're fired on, we will respond and we will respond with lethal efficiency.

104
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:21,900
The assets supporting this project, by the way, include guided missile destroyers, over

105
00:05:22,260 --> 00:05:27,960
100 land and sea-based aircraft, multi-domain unmanned platforms, and 15,000 of the finest

106
00:05:28,540 --> 00:05:30,680
military service members on the planet.

107
00:05:30,900 --> 00:05:38,280
These forces have already destroyed, as I told you earlier, I thought it was six, it's seven Iranian fastboats that fail to heed our warnings.

108
00:05:38,300 --> 00:05:41,640
And by fastboats, we're talking about, you know, some of these things look like Boston whalers.

109
00:05:41,880 --> 00:05:43,520
Okay, so these are not like Navy ships.

110
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:47,080
But nonetheless, they come fast at these boats, try to swarm them, try to harm them.

111
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:48,220
We're not going to let that happen.

112
00:05:48,220 --> 00:05:52,860
So seven of them now sit at the bottom of the sea, along with, by the way, the rest of Iran's Navy.

113
00:05:52,980 --> 00:05:54,500
That's where you can find their Navy today.

114
00:05:54,900 --> 00:06:00,120
And we're going to continue to systematically clear this passageway through the Straits to restore freedom of navigation.

115
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:00,280
Okay.

116
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:07,120
Now, while this project steadily progresses, Operation Epic Fury, economic fury, I'm sorry,

117
00:06:07,140 --> 00:06:13,780
continues to impose maximum pressure on the Iranian regime and what remains of their already frail economy.

118
00:06:13,860 --> 00:06:20,080
Today, inflation in Iran is 70 percent, and their currency is in total and complete freefall.

119
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:22,080
U.S. sanctions enforcement is stepping up.

120
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:29,360
It's moving in lockstep with the naval blockade to degrade Iran's capacity to generate, to move, and repatriate revenue.

121
00:06:29,840 --> 00:06:33,580
It directly targets the regime's primary revenue lifelines.

122
00:06:33,860 --> 00:06:40,340
The blockade alone is costing Iran as much as $500 million a day in lost revenue.

123
00:06:40,840 --> 00:06:47,280
90% of total Iranian trade has been halted, causing permanent damage to Iran's oil infrastructure as wells are forced

124
00:06:47,180 --> 00:06:51,940
to shut in. Again, all of this is in response to their piracy.

125
00:06:52,060 --> 00:06:53,780
It cannot be that you have these straits.

126
00:06:53,980 --> 00:06:57,500
and they blow up any ship that moves and the only ships they get to go through are theirs.

127
00:06:57,500 --> 00:07:00,260
You can't have a situation in which the straits are close to everyone else,

128
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:03,000
but they benefit from the piracy. That can't happen.

129
00:07:03,020 --> 00:07:06,540
That's why the blockade is in place, and that's why these sanctions are crippling them.

130
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:12,540
Any foreign financial, by the way, Treasury is now identifying and cutting off every dollar of revenue

131
00:07:12,540 --> 00:07:13,840
that's flowing to this regime.

132
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:19,060
And so, look, any foreign financial institution or commercial actor that enables Iran's sanctions evasion

133
00:07:19,260 --> 00:07:22,920
is going to face secondary sanctions exposure and a loss of access

134
00:07:23,020 --> 00:07:25,260
to the U.S. financial system.

135
00:07:25,260 --> 00:07:26,580
As President Trump has said,

136
00:07:26,580 --> 00:07:27,980
and the facts clearly bear out,

137
00:07:28,260 --> 00:07:31,080
the United States of America holds all the cards.

138
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:33,020
There is no scenario here

139
00:07:33,240 --> 00:07:36,200
in which if they decide to join a ladder of escalation,

140
00:07:36,200 --> 00:07:37,600
they wind up getting the last say.

141
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:38,820
But our preference...

142
00:07:39,080 --> 00:07:44,100
is for these straits to be opened to the way they're supposed to be open, back to the way it was.

143
00:07:44,100 --> 00:07:49,120
Anyone can use it. No mines in the water. Nobody paying tolls. That's what we have to get back to,

144
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:53,200
and that's the goal here. Every day the conflict continues, however, our leverage on Iran will

145
00:07:53,380 --> 00:07:57,880
continue to increase, and their position will continue to weaken, especially as the blockade

146
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:02,380
really begins to bite in conjunction with the sanctions. So look, the times come for Iran to make

147
00:08:02,420 --> 00:08:06,500
a sensible choice, and it's not easy for them to do that, obviously, because they have a fracture

148
00:08:06,820 --> 00:08:08,340
in their own leadership system and...

149
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:17,040
Apart from that, I mean, the top people in that government are, to say the least, you know, they're insane in the brain.

150
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:21,520
And so we need to address that, and it's difficult because it's hard to get past that in their system.

151
00:08:21,640 --> 00:08:24,800
But it's important for them to make a sensible choice and the one that's right for their people.

152
00:08:25,160 --> 00:08:28,740
The president, our president, has proven time and again that his preference is peace.

153
00:08:29,160 --> 00:08:34,020
But Iran must accept the reality of this situation and come to the negotiation table

154
00:08:34,380 --> 00:08:37,080
and accept terms that are good for them but ultimately good for the world.

155
00:08:37,260 --> 00:08:40,960
The diplomatic path, if there's a real diplomatic path, I'm not sure what's going to be one,

156
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:43,860
but if there's a real diplomatic path and we continue to explore it,

157
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:46,000
Steve and Jared are working on that very hard.

158
00:08:46,040 --> 00:08:49,100
If there is one there, it could be one that leads them to reconstruction,

159
00:08:49,100 --> 00:08:52,860
to prosperity and to stability and to not posing a threat to the world.

160
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:57,940
The alternative is growing isolation, economic collapse, and ultimately total defeat.

161
00:08:58,260 --> 00:09:00,560
I know what the right choice is for Iran.

162
00:09:00,580 --> 00:09:03,380
I hope that the people over there making decisions will make the right one.

163
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:06,880
The last point I would make, and it really is important for them to understand this,

164
00:09:07,120 --> 00:09:11,080
is they really shouldn't test the will of the United States, at least not under President Donald Trump.

165
00:09:11,360 --> 00:09:14,500
He has proven time and again that he will back up what he says.

166
00:09:14,700 --> 00:09:16,760
And if they test him, ultimately they will lose.

167
00:09:17,240 --> 00:09:20,260
The hard way, the easy way, the long way, the short way, they will lose.

168
00:09:20,840 --> 00:09:23,460
And with that, it's time for your questions.

169
00:09:23,840 --> 00:09:24,040
Secretary.

170
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:24,640
Secretary.

171
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:26,400
Catherine, why don't we start with you first?

172
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:28,720
By Catherine Herich, I'm an independent journalist.

173
00:09:28,960 --> 00:09:40,240
Secretary Rubio, have you seen any recent indications that Iran is willing to give up this nuclear weapons program that is credible, verifiable, and that would lead to an immediate de-escalation?

174
00:09:40,380 --> 00:09:41,920
Well, look.

175
00:09:42,320 --> 00:09:44,460
This is a long-standing problem for them, right?

176
00:09:44,460 --> 00:09:47,520
I mean, they have wanted, they have always said they don't want a nuclear weapon.

177
00:09:47,560 --> 00:09:48,780
That's because they've always said that.

178
00:09:48,780 --> 00:09:49,720
They just don't mean it.

179
00:09:49,720 --> 00:09:51,460
And why do you say, well, how do you know they don't mean it?

180
00:09:51,460 --> 00:09:54,320
Well, we don't mean it because they do all, they're doing all the things and historically

181
00:09:54,440 --> 00:09:57,900
have tried to do all the things that you do if you want a nuclear weapons program.

182
00:09:57,980 --> 00:10:02,660
For example, they innovate and try to innovate long-range delivery missiles that now in

183
00:10:02,680 --> 00:10:04,740
some cases are capable of reaching much of Europe.

184
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:10,740
They build these large underground centrifuges for enrichment activity.

185
00:10:10,400 --> 00:10:17,180
There are many, there are countries in the world that are involved in the enrichment business, but these guys do it in mountains and in caves and in hiding.

186
00:10:17,380 --> 00:10:30,360
They've always had secret components of their nuclear program undisclosed to the world, and we know for a fact that they retain highly enriched uranium at 60% that they did so, and that has no civilian use, none, zero whatsoever.

187
00:10:30,560 --> 00:10:37,680
So they have an opportunity here to agree to something that will make it clear that they're not interested in a new one thing is to say we don't want a nuclear weapon.

188
00:10:37,700 --> 00:10:40,180
Another thing is to do the things that prove you don't want a nuclear weapon.

189
00:10:40,340 --> 00:10:48,400
By the way, if what Iran wants is a civilian nuclear program for power plants and stuff like that, there are a lot of countries in the world that have that and they don't enrich.

190
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:52,120
They import the enriched material.

191
00:10:52,300 --> 00:10:55,380
They could have that if that's what they wanted, but they're not acting like that's what they wanted.

192
00:10:55,500 --> 00:10:58,860
They're acting like they want a military nuclear program.

193
00:10:59,040 --> 00:11:00,380
That's unacceptable.

194
00:11:00,380 --> 00:11:05,600
So that's the process we're engaged in now, to create, that's the object of this diplomacy,

195
00:11:05,740 --> 00:11:10,400
is to come up with some level of understanding about what are the topics that they've agreed to negotiate on.

196
00:11:10,460 --> 00:11:14,420
We don't have to have the actual agreement written out, and one day this is highly complex and highly technical.

197
00:11:14,440 --> 00:11:25,160
But we have to have a diplomatic solution that is very clear about the topics that they are willing to negotiate on and the extent and the concessions they're willing to make at the front end in order to make those talks worthwhile.

198
00:11:25,460 --> 00:11:28,020
That's what Stephen Jarrett and the whole team is working on.

199
00:11:28,020 --> 00:11:29,200
And I hope to have good news on it.

200
00:11:29,240 --> 00:11:30,640
That's the outcome we would prefer.

201
00:11:30,660 --> 00:11:33,640
That's the outcome we would have preferred a year ago.

202
00:11:33,660 --> 00:11:36,520
That's the outcome I think most of us would have preferred a long time ago.

203
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:39,580
But that's not the option they've given us given their activity.

204
00:11:39,580 --> 00:11:40,280
All right, can I?

205
00:11:40,280 --> 00:11:42,000
There's no way I can figure out who to call on.

206
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,560
I'm just going to like press right in the middle, right there, you right there.

207
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:45,220
Yeah.

208
00:11:45,220 --> 00:11:46,320
In the back row.

209
00:11:46,360 --> 00:11:47,820
Thank you, Mr. President.

210
00:11:47,820 --> 00:11:49,280
I'll go to you next.

211
00:11:49,280 --> 00:11:49,460
All right, go ahead.

212
00:11:49,460 --> 00:11:52,200
Thank you, Secretary Rubio.

213
00:11:52,200 --> 00:11:53,060
Keep going right.

214
00:11:53,060 --> 00:11:54,080
Welcome to the White House.

215
00:11:54,540 --> 00:11:55,240
What's that?

216
00:11:55,240 --> 00:11:56,220
You talking to me, sir?

217
00:11:56,220 --> 00:11:56,860
Yeah, to you.

218
00:11:56,860 --> 00:11:57,000
Yes, sir.

219
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:57,960
Thank you.

220
00:11:57,960 --> 00:12:00,880
I have two questions on two separate issues that come under...

221
00:12:00,880 --> 00:12:02,080
Do they get two questions for these?

222
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:02,840
Two questions.

223
00:12:02,840 --> 00:12:03,840
There's a lot of people in here.

224
00:12:03,840 --> 00:12:05,280
That is all under your umbrella.

225
00:12:05,300 --> 00:12:05,620
All right.

226
00:12:05,620 --> 00:12:07,700
Well, you can ask me two questions.

227
00:12:07,700 --> 00:12:08,460
I'll give you one answer.

228
00:12:08,460 --> 00:12:09,540
Thank you very much.

